  "content": {
    "title": "Is there mail on Good Friday? Are banks closed? See open, closed list",
    "text": "April 2, 2026Updated April 3, 2026, 10:45 a.m. ETWith Easter Sunday set for April 5, that means it's Good Friday.While many retail stores will be closed Sunday for the Christian holiday, not everything will shut down for Good Friday.Here's a look at what will stay open and what will close for Good Friday:Is Good Friday a holiday?Good Friday is the day in which Christian and Catholic churches across the world commemorate the crucifixion and death of Jesus Christ. \"From the early days of Christianity, Good Friday was observed as a day of sorrow, penance, and fasting,\" Britannica notes.When is Good Friday?Good Friday is observed the Friday before Easter Sunday. This year, it's on April 3.What is Easter?Easter falls on Sunday, April 5, this year, and it celebrates the resurrection of Jesus Christ and follows a 40-day period known as Lent. The date the holidays are celebrated changes depending on the year because Easter is one of several \"moveable feasts\" in the liturgical year, according to the History Channel. Other examples of \"moveable feasts\" include Ash Wednesday and Palm Sunday.When is Easter in 2026?All churches celebrate Easter on April 5 this year.What is closed on Good Friday?While not a federal holiday, Good Friday is recognized as a state public holiday in some states including Delaware and New Jersey.Most schools also will be closed since Good Friday falls on spring break for many school districts.Is there mail on Good Friday?The U.S. Postal Service will be open and offer mail services while UPS and FedEx will continue deliveries.Is the market closed on Good friday?The Nasdaq and the New York Stock Exchange will be closed.Are banks open on Good Friday 2026?Most banks will be open for regular business hours.Are stores and restaurants closed on Good Friday?Retail stores and restaurants will remain open with regular hours.(This story was updated because an earlier version included an inaccuracy.)",
